  "prediction": "The report provides essential resources for congressional staff seeking to understand legislative procedure and processes in the House and Senate. The report recommends visiting the official websites of the House and Senate for legislative process overviews, including the House's \"Legislative Process\" website (http://clerk.house.gov/legislative/legprocess.aspx) and the Senate's \"Legislative Process\" website (http://www.senate.gov/pagelayout/legislative/d_three_sections_with_teasers/process.htm).\n\nThe report also lists several key publications to help congressional staff gain a better understanding of legislative procedure. These include:\n\n1. House Rules and Manual\n2. House Practice: A Guide to the Rules, Precedents, and Procedures of the House\n3. Procedure in the U.S. House of Representatives\n4. Senate Manual\n5. Riddick's Senate Procedure\n6. Senate Cloture Rule\n7. Our American Government\n8. The Constitution of the United States of America As Amended\n9. Constitution Annotated\n10. Enactment of a Law: Procedural Steps in the Legislative Process\n11. How Our Laws Are Made\n\nThe report also mentions several CRS reports and programs available to congressional staff for further learning on legislative procedure. These resources can be accessed on the CRS website (http://www.crs.gov) and include fact sheets, reports, and training programs.\n\nIn summary, the report offers congressional staff a comprehensive list of resources, including websites, publications, and CRS reports and programs, to help them gain a solid understanding of legislative procedure and processes in the House and Senate.",
  "scored_prediction": "The report provides essential resources for congressional staff seeking to understand legislative procedure and processes in the House and Senate. The report recommends visiting the official websites of the House and Senate for legislative process overviews, including the House's \"Legislative Process\" website (http://clerk.house.gov/legislative/legprocess.aspx) and the Senate's \"Legislative Process\" website (http://www.senate.gov/pagelayout/legislative/d_three_sections_with_teasers/process.htm).\n\nThe report also lists several key publications to help congressional staff gain a better understanding of legislative procedure. These include:\n\n1. House Rules and Manual\n2. House Practice: A Guide to the Rules, Precedents, and Procedures of the House\n3. Procedure in the U.S. House of Representatives\n4. Senate Manual\n5. Riddick's Senate Procedure\n6. Senate Cloture Rule\n7. Our American Government\n8. The Constitution of the United States of America As Amended\n9. Constitution Annotated\n10. Enactment of a Law: Procedural Steps in the Legislative Process\n11. How Our Laws Are Made\n\nThe report also mentions several CRS reports and programs available to congressional staff for further learning on legislative procedure. These resources can be accessed on the CRS website (http://www.crs.gov) and include fact sheets, reports, and training programs.\n\nIn summary, the report offers congressional staff a comprehensive list of resources, including websites, publications, and CRS reports and programs, to help them gain a solid understanding of legislative procedure and processes in the House and Senate.",
  "score": 18.713449910194598,
  "answers": [
    "Written for congressional staff, this report identifies and provides details on how to obtain information on legislative procedures and process in the House and Senate. It provides references to selected CRS products and offers information on the CRS legislative institutes. A listing of selected supplementary materials is also provided. This report will be updated as new information is available."
  ],
